> **Not** `SSEClientTransport` — that is the legacy SSE-only transport and uses a different handshake. The brain implements the MCP Streamable HTTP spec (POST `/mcp` + GET `/mcp` SSE).

Source: the user, after Phase 13. Career on the map is full of people and cities that aren't theirs. They want to see how the nodes relate, to edit the relationships and the topics things belong to, and to put the Trump monitor under the Niche Intelligence Agency.

Decisions taken in the discussion (2026-10-09):
1. **The Trump Benefit Daily Monitor belongs to the Niche Intelligence Agency (NIA).** NIA is a broad topic, and the user accepts that.
2. **Relationships: both kinds, co-mentions first.** Ship a view built from shared notes now, then typed relations as a later phase.
3. **Editable:**
   - a note's topics;
   - keeping an entity out of a topic;
   - relationships between entities;
   - the topics themselves.

- **Entity drawer, a Reference block:**
  - the label, the Wikidata description and the article's first paragraph, linked to Wikipedia and Wikidata;
  - a chip saying suggested / confirmed;
  - confirm, "not this", "choose another" (the cached candidates), paste a Wikipedia URL, and "Look up";
  - "No article found (checked 9 Oct)" when there is none, never a blank.
- **Where decisions are made:** wherever an entity is decided, its one-line Wikidata description shows beside its name:
  - the relationship review list;
  - the pair view;
  - the merge candidates.

  This is the user's actual need: deciding about an unfamiliar name without leaving the page.
- **Topics:** most topics are the user's own groupings and have no article. So a topic gets an optional `reference` URL set in the topic form, or `reference:` in its YAML, and the topic drawer shows it. There is no automatic lookup for topics.

- **Declared:**
  - `topic:` (a `topics/*.yaml` name, or none) and `kind:` (`monitor` · `brief` · `learning` · `maintenance` · `dataset`), set in `schedules/*.yaml`, on the ScheduledTask node, and in the HBI form;
  - the seeder validates `topic` against the declared topics;
  - starting values for all 28 are drafted for the user to review, as with topics in 4b.
- **Computed:**
  - the 30-day topic mix of the notes a schedule's runs produced, from the lineage `clients/work.rs` already reconstructs (schedule → task → jobs → notes);
  - shown next to the declared topic, with a mismatch flagged and a suggestion where none is declared (the same declared-vs-computed pattern as the architecture view).
- **List and timeline:**
  - group by topic, kind or none (remembered);
  - collapsible groups with on/off counts;
  - "no topic" is a group of its own, never hidden.

The sidebar collapses to an icon rail (~52 px):
- labels move into tooltips;
- the badge stays;
- the choice is remembered per browser.

Built and deployed:
- **Items run as agent work** (`services/item_dispatch.rs`):
  - when due, an item assigned to an agent gets a Task (`SPAWNED`) owning `run_agent` → `manage_todo deliver` → `update_task`;
  - the result is stored as a note, posted into the item's thread, and waits in `awaiting_review`.
- **Asks first** (`items::side_effects`; delayed-prompt Phase 2): an agent holding a tool that acts outside the graph waits in `awaiting_approval`. `knowledge-worker`, `code-analyst` and `schedule-author` run on their own; the other eight ask, each with its reason.
- **Guardrails:** approvals are single-use, at most 3 runs an item and 3 per poll, runs are claimed atomically, and a failed run is retried or handed back with the reason.
- **The user's controls** (REST; the brain may not assign or approve): hand to an agent (agent, model, prompt), approve run, take back, approve result, send back with a comment.
- **`dispatch_due_todos` + hourly `item-dispatch.yaml`** (delayed-prompt Phase 3).
- **"Make it a schedule":** `schedule-author` drafts; the draft is created disabled, linked `BECAME`, and repaired once if rejected; turning it on closes the item.
- **The Daily Brief's "Your items" block** (delayed-prompt Phase 4). The daily Todo Review chat is retired.
- **HBI:**
  - Feed rows have "Hand to an agent", and approve / take back / result / send back on items waiting for the user;
  - the item drawer has an Agent section (runs, last result, last comment, hand-over, make it a schedule with a model picker);
  - the agent picker says whether each agent runs on its own or asks first.

**Found while building:**
- **`researcher` must not be overridden to `read_only`.** The delayed-prompt plan proposed it when `researcher` held only `http_request`; it now holds `mcp_call` too, which can reach aidevboard's `apply_to_job`. It asks first, and the Sodium run showed why asking costs little: one click.
- **The HBI saved schedules without the argument-schema check.** "Make it a schedule" first produced a draft whose `reason` step had `prompt` instead of the required `question`, a step that would fail every run. REST saves now run the same hard check as `manage_scheduled_task`.
- **`schedule-author` on the local model failed this item twice** (its draft, then its repair), now rejected rather than saved. `gpt-oss:120b-cloud` drafted a clean one first time, hence the model picker on the button.
- **Deleting a linked schedule failed:** a plain `DELETE` refuses a node with edges, so it is now `DETACH DELETE`.
- **Assign ignored the due date** while its button said "runs when due". It now waits for the date; approve and send-back run at once.

**Verified live (the done-condition):**
- **"Research Sodium batteries"** (due Oct 31) handed to `researcher`:
  - `awaiting_approval`, with "can act outside the brain (http_request, mcp_call)" posted in its thread;
  - the user pressed Approve run;
  - run 1 took about a minute: `search_notes`, `search_web` ×2, `reason` ×2, `store_note`, with no `http_request` or `mcp_call`;
  - a cited brief (how they work, how they're made, CATL / Mana / Alsym and others, cost, availability) went into the thread and note 37c8e0db, `awaiting_review`, and the user approved it to done.
- **"Make it a schedule"** on it produced `weekly-sodium-battery-research`: disabled, weekly, `search_web` → `reason` (`context: {{_prev}}`) → `store_note` (`source_record`), linked `BECAME`, and passing the audit.
- **Also checked** with throwaway items, deleted afterwards:
  - a `knowledge-worker` item started without approval and delivered;
  - send back reran it with the previous answer and the comment (run 2);
  - approve made it done;
  - turning a linked draft on closed its item;
  - a simulated failed third run went to review with its reason, and no fourth run started;
  - the dispatch schedule's run reported "0 started … 0 reconciled".

**Tests:** 868 Rust lib tests (4 new) and 95 vitest.

**Not built:** an "agent:auto" choice in the hand-over picker. It always asks first, as the plan's open question 3 advised.

Built and deployed:
- **Every note's provenance is decided by who writes it** (`knowledge::effective_provenance`):
  - the brain's own work (anything under a route context: queue jobs, chat turns, sub-agents) is `synthesis_inference`, and can't claim `user_input` even when a model passes it;
  - an outside MCP client's own call (`CLIENT_CALL`, `mcp/server.rs`) is `user_input`.
  - **New value `system_record`** for records the brain's code builds (scheduler dispatches, claim evidence, code-version notes). Calling them LLM synthesis would have been the opposite mislabel.
  - Recall reads a missing value as `unrecorded`, not `user_input`. The HBI's fallback note path now writes `user_input` explicitly.
- **Data fix applied** (`scripts/fix_note_provenance.cypher`, run after the deploy): 5,942 notes became `system_record` and 6,327 `synthesis_inference`. Only the 13 résumé notes are still `user_input`, and none has no provenance. Each relabelled note keeps `provenance_was`.
- **Claims must stand alone at extraction** (`claims::standalone_problems`, mechanical):
  - the model declares `about: [{name, what}]`;
  - every name must be in the claim, and be a name, not a vague noun;
  - every bare "the + vague noun" must be declared and named;
  - the claim may not open with a pronoun.

  A failing claim gets one repair (`repair_claim`) and is otherwise dropped, listed in the tool's `dropped`. Stored claims carry `about`, `source_url`, `source_title` and `source_outlet`. The topic classifier reads the source title with the claim.
  - **Measured on the local model** (Gemma E4B, which does all live extraction), 8 real sources: 21 claims stored, 2 repaired, 2 dropped. One dropped claim was a meta-claim ("The video discusses …"); the other said "seven financial institutions" without saying where.
- **`rewrite-claims`** (`services/claim_rewrite.rs`, CLI):
  - one cloud call per source note;
  - a rewrite replaces a claim only if it stands alone **and** loses no number or name (`rewrite_losses`, which folds Unicode hyphens and spaces first);
  - one targeted repair, otherwise the text is kept and the claim marked `ambiguous` with the reason;
  - the old text goes to `content_v1`, and the claim is re-embedded and re-queued for topics;
  - a snapshot is taken first, and a stopped run resumes.
  - The claim drawer shows **About**, the ambiguity reason, and the original text of a rewritten claim.
- **Tests:** 846 Rust lib tests (14 new), 82 vitest.

**Dry run on 50 claims across writers** (cloud, nothing written): 53 claims from 10 sources.
- **Outcome:** 30 rewritten, 20 already stood alone, 2 ambiguous, 1 kept by the meaning guard.
- **Iterations to get there:**
  - the first run kept 12 for typography (non-breaking hyphens, number ranges);
  - the next lost acronyms (US → United States) and dates (→ "September 28");
  - both were fixed in the comparison and the prompt.
- **The Paragon claim (889d47f8)** now reads: "Paragon Solutions' CEO Andrew Boyd said that Paragon Solutions lacks a mechanism to verify customer misuse of its spyware or to deploy a 'kill switch' to disable the Paragon Solutions spyware." That needed the rule "if the source gives no name, say whose it is". The source never names the product.
- **Not caught by any check:** "admitted" became "said". Verbs that soften are invisible to a mechanical guard, which is why the user reads the dry-run report before the full run.

**Full run, 2026-10-09** (after the user approved the dry run): 4,510 claims from 1,054 sources in 87 minutes, 0 calls failed. Snapshot first: `snapshot_pre_claim_rewrite_20261009_040444.json.gz`.
- **Outcome:**
  - 2,712 rewritten;
  - 1,578 already stood alone;
  - 152 ambiguous;
  - 43 kept by the meaning guard;
  - 25 with no source note left.

  That makes 220 claims marked `ambiguous`, each with its reason. The rewritten claims dropped `topics_version`, so the idle topic pass classifies them again.
- **Before the run, `judge` counted a typography-only change as a rewrite.** It now compares lowercase letters and digits only, so "2024" vs "2024." and "64%" vs "64 %" count as unchanged.
- **`eval-retrieval`:** recall@10 went from 0.920 to 0.880, and MRR from 0.757 to 0.780.
  - One case improved, from rank 3 to rank 1 (Leavitt).
  - One was lost: "Can Reticulum firmware run directly on Meshtastic LoRa radios?" It was a hit at rank 10, at the very edge, and is now outside the top 30.
  - Its target claim (77b8c4ed) was not changed; it already stood alone. It is outranked by rewritten neighbours that now name Reticulum and Meshtastic outright ("A bridge (such as a Raspberry Pi) can now link the Meshtastic application/firmware with the Reticulum networking protocol"). Those neighbours answer the same question.
  - On 25 cases a single case is 0.04, so this is read as a re-rank among relevant claims, not damage. The snapshot restores the old text if it proves otherwise.

Not built: a check that catches a missing location or target ("AI agents were deployed", with no target named).

**Problem:** `ForceGraph2D` renders at a fixed internal size instead of filling its flex parent. The graph either clips or leaves dead space.

**Fix:** Use a `ResizeObserver` (or `useLayoutEffect` + `useRef` on the container `div`) to measure the container's actual pixel dimensions and pass them as explicit `width` and `height` props to `ForceGraph2D`.

```tsx
// GraphPanel.tsx — inside the component
const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
const [dims, setDims] = useState({ w: 800, h: 600 });

useLayoutEffect(() => {
  if (!containerRef.current) return;
  const ro = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
    const { width, height } = entry.contentRect;
    setDims({ w: Math.floor(width), h: Math.floor(height) });
  });
  ro.observe(containerRef.current);
  return () => ro.disconnect();
}, []);

// Then:
<div ref={containerRef} className="graph-canvas-wrapper">
  <ForceGraph2D width={dims.w} height={dims.h} ... />
</div>
```

**File:** `hbi-frontend/src/components/graph/GraphPanel.tsx`

